      "boxes": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "type": "text",
          "x": 60,
          "y": 60,
          "w": 340,
          "h": 300,
          "title": "I. The Shore",
          "text": "After the tide withdraws, the beach is scattered with the loosened knots of the world: a crown of kelp, a pinwheel with a broken wing, shells that no one has named. The boy gathers them one by one and holds each up to the light.\nFar away, his mother is calling. The wind unravels her voice before it can become words. He does not turn. In his palm, just now, a small whelk is slowly winding back the memory of the sea.",
          "vert": false,
          "color": "sky",
          "link": null
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "type": "text",
          "x": 480,
          "y": 60,
          "w": 340,
          "h": 300,
          "title": "II. Glass Weather",
          "text": "The lighthouse keeper measured weather with glass. Seven colored panes stood on his sill, and by the way morning light broke through them he named the day's wind. \"It will fall apart from the south,\" he would say — and the sea obeyed.\nWhich comes first, the boy asked, the glass or the weather? The old man only laughed, set the bluest pane in the boy's palm and said: \"Keep it. On gray days, this will be your clear sky.\"",
          "vert": false,
          "color": "none",
          "link": null
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "type": "text",
          "x": 60,
          "y": 560,
          "w": 340,
          "h": 300,
          "title": "III. The Fire Carrier",
          "text": "Someone walked the night beach carrying fire. Not a fishing torch, not the leavings of a festival — an old promised fire, carried from cape to cape without ever being let die.\n\"Fire can only travel at walking speed,\" the carrier said. \"Run, and it goes out. The more a night wants hurrying, the slower you walk.\"\nThe boy knew a similar fire deep in his own chest: small, easily put out, never yet shown to anyone.",
          "vert": false,
          "color": "coral",
          "link": null
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "type": "text",
          "x": 480,
          "y": 560,
          "w": 340,
          "h": 300,
          "title": "IV. Homeward",
          "text": "A single white sail is born out of the horizon. The boy knows it is his father's boat even from here — the tilt of the sail resembles his father's crooked way of smiling.\nRunning down the beach, he counts the day's gatherings: a whelk, a blue pane of glass, and a few things still too early to put into words.\nThe sea wind pushes at his back. A summer is about to end.",
          "vert": false,
          "color": "mint",
          "link": null
        },
        {
          "id": 11,
          "type": "text",
          "x": 900,
          "y": 60,
          "w": 320,
          "h": 200,
          "title": "Characters",
          "text": "The boy — ten years old. A gatherer; wants to hold the world in his palm.\nThe mother — the one who calls. Knows about rain that has not yet arrived.\nThe keeper — measures weather with glass. A giver of clear skies.\nThe fire carrier — the philosophy of walking speed. Identity revealed in the last chapter.",
          "vert": false,
          "color": "lemon",
          "link": null
        },
        {
          "id": 12,
          "type": "text",
          "x": 900,
          "y": 560,
          "w": 320,
          "h": 200,
          "title": "Notes",
          "text": "A cycle about picking things up. Each chapter leaves one object in the boy's palm (whelk → blue glass → fire → words → sail). In the end, every gathered thing turns out to be a preparation for parting.",
          "vert": false,
          "color": "sky",
          "link": null
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "name": "Fragments",
      "view": {
        "x": 80,
